(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-acid-base-disorders-ii-quiz"]={"title":"Acid-Base Disorders II Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which cause of a decreased anion gap requires the gap to be corrected before it is interpreted?","choices":["Lithium ingestion","Bromide intoxication","Raised serum albumin","Low serum albumin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypoalbuminemia lowers the anion gap because albumin is an unmeasured anion, and the gap must then be corrected by adding 2.5 for each 1 g/dL of albumin below 4.5 g/dL. A corrected gap can reveal a raised gap hidden by the low albumin. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 7, 8, 48"},{"q":"For each 1 g/dL that serum albumin falls below 4.5 g/dL, how much is added to the anion gap?","choices":["1 mmol/L","2 mmol/L","2.5 mmol/L","3.5 mmol/L"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The anion gap must be corrected when albumin is abnormal: add 2.5 mmol/L for each 1 g/dL that serum albumin falls below 4.5 g/dL. An albumin of 2.5 g/dL, 2 g/dL below, adds 5 to the measured gap. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is listed as a cause of respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Anxiety","Kyphoscoliosis","Sedative use","Diarrhea"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory alkalosis is a pH above 7.45 with a decreased carbon dioxide. Listed causes include anxiety, hypoxia, high altitude, decreased lung compliance from interstitial fibrosis, and catastrophic events of the central nervous system such as intracerebral hemorrhage. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 32, 38, 39, 46"},{"q":"In acidosis, which shift explains the hyperkalemia?","choices":["Hydrogen enters cells as potassium leaves","Hydrogen leaves cells as potassium enters","Hemoglobin releases potassium with oxygen","Bicarbonate carries potassium into serum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In acidosis hydrogen ions shift from the extracellular fluid into the cells, and potassium shifts from inside the cells out to the extracellular fluid. The potassium that moves out raises the serum level, resulting in hyperkalemia. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In metabolic alkalosis with hypokalemia, how are potassium deficits handled?","choices":["Replaced fast to a high-normal level","Withheld until the pH is normal","Ignored, since the level is normal","Replaced if needed, without overcorrecting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alkalosis shifts potassium from the extracellular fluid into the cells, which lowers the serum level. Management treats the underlying condition and treats fluid and potassium deficits if needed, with the warning not to overcorrect. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 52, 54, 55"},{"q":"Which pair of primary disorders cannot coexist in one patient?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis and respiratory acidosis","Respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A patient cannot have more than one primary respiratory disorder at a time. Two metabolic disorders can coexist, and a metabolic disorder can pair with either respiratory disorder, which is how the six recognized mixed patterns arise. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 28, 29, 30"},{"q":"What does inadequate or excessive compensation for a disorder point to?","choices":["A simple disorder in its chronic phase","A normal acid-base state","A low serum albumin","A mixed acid-base disorder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Expected compensation is calculated for the primary disorder, and inadequate or excessive compensation points to a mixed acid-base disorder, a second primary disturbance pulling the values away from the expected response. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 26"},{"q":"Which clinical setting classically produces metabolic acidosis with metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["Liver disease with diuretics","Sepsis with lactic acidosis","Uremia with vomiting","Diarrhea with lactic acidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Uremia is a raised-gap metabolic acidosis cause and vomiting, by loss of gastric acid, is a metabolic alkalosis cause. The pH and bicarbonate can look nearly normal, and the clue is a change in gap much larger than the change in bicarbonate. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 44, 53"},{"q":"Which clinical setting classically produces metabolic alkalosis with respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis","Liver disease with diuretics","Uremia with vomiting","Severe pneumonia with pulmonary edema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Liver disease with diuretics is the classic example. Diuretics are a saline-responsive cause of metabolic alkalosis, and the carbon dioxide does not rise as predicted, leaving the pH higher than expected. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 30, 53"},{"q":"Over what time course does the renal mechanism adjust plasma bicarbonate?","choices":["Seconds to minutes","Minutes to an hour","Hours to days","Weeks to months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The renal mechanism controls plasma bicarbonate by excreting or retaining filtered bicarbonate, and it works over hours to days. That is why chronic respiratory acidosis shows its larger bicarbonate rise only after 24 to 48 hours of sustained carbon dioxide elevation. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 19, 34"},{"q":"A chemistry panel shows sodium, chloride and bicarbonate each within its normal range. What follows for the anion gap?","choices":["It can still be abnormal","It must be normal too","It cannot be calculated","It needs albumin correction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The anion gap can be abnormal even when sodium, chloride and bicarbonate are each within the normal range, so it has to be calculated rather than assumed. That is why calculating the gap is its own step after the primary disorder is identified. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 8, 26"},{"q":"When an arterial blood gas is read to find the primary disorder, which value is examined first?","choices":["The anion gap","The pH","The bicarbonate","The carbon dioxide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pH comes first: below 7.35 is acidemia and above 7.45 is alkalemia. Only then are the carbon dioxide and bicarbonate checked to see which one matches the pH direction, naming the disorder respiratory or metabolic. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 56, 58, 59, 63"},{"q":"In a simple acid-base disorder, how far does compensation move the pH?","choices":["Fully back to a pH of 7.40","Past normal to the opposite side","Not at all until the cause is treated","Toward normal, never fully back"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The compensatory response will not fully correct the pH disturbance; the underlying condition must be corrected to restore pH balance. A pH sitting right on normal therefore raises the question of a second, opposing disorder. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which pair cannot occur together as a mixed acid-base disorder?","choices":["Respiratory acidosis and respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis and respiratory acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A patient cannot have more than one primary respiratory disorder, because carbon dioxide cannot be high and low at once. Several metabolic disorders can coexist, and a metabolic disorder can pair with a respiratory one. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 28, 29, 30"},{"q":"Which clinical setting classically produces metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Sepsis with lactic acidosis","Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease on diuretics","Diarrhea with lactic acidosis","Severe pneumonia or pulmonary edema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe pneumonia and pulmonary edema are listed causes of respiratory acidosis, and they pair with a high or normal gap metabolic acidosis. The key finding is a prevailing carbon dioxide above the predicted value. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 28, 30, 32"},{"q":"Carbon dioxide rises from 40 to 70 mm Hg within the past hour, from a baseline bicarbonate of 24 mEq/L. The acute bicarbonate rise is 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg and the chronic rise is 3.5 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg. What bicarbonate is expected?","choices":["24 mEq/L","27 mEq/L","30 mEq/L","34.5 mEq/L"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A rise within the past hour is acute, so bicarbonate climbs 1 mEq/L for each 10 mm Hg. Carbon dioxide rose 30 mm Hg, three steps of 10, so bicarbonate rises 3: 24 + 3 = 27 mEq/L. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 24, 33, 34, 35"},{"q":"Which part of management differs in chronic respiratory acidosis compared with acute respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Reviewing the medication list","Clearing airway secretions","Careful oxygen administration","Treating the underlying condition"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic respiratory acidosis is treated much like acute, but oxygen is given carefully: a rapidly falling carbon dioxide in a patient with chronic respiratory acidosis may trigger complications of alkalosis, such as cardiac arrhythmias, reduced cerebral perfusion and seizures. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 36, 37"},{"q":"Which pattern marks metabolic alkalosis combined with respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Carbon dioxide below predicted, high pH","Carbon dioxide as predicted, high pH","Carbon dioxide below predicted, normal pH","Carbon dioxide above predicted, normal pH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The key is a carbon dioxide higher than predicted for the metabolic alkalosis, with a normal pH: the alkalosis pushes the pH up and the respiratory acidosis pulls it down. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease on diuretics is the example. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 28"},{"q":"Laboratory results show pH 7.43 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 22 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); anion gap 22 (normal 6 to 12). Which disorders are present?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis with full compensation","Metabolic acidosis and respiratory acidosis","Primary respiratory alkalosis alone","Metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate 14 with a gap of 22 is a raised-gap metabolic acidosis. Winter formula: 1.5 x 14 + 8 = 29, range 27 to 31. The measured 22 is below that range, so a concurrent respiratory alkalosis is present, which is why the pH sits at 7.43. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 28, 49, 26"},{"q":"Laboratory results show pH 7.35 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 94 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 16 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which disorders are present?","choices":["Metabolic acidosis and respiratory acidosis","Metabolic acidosis and metabolic alkalosis","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis alone","Raised-gap and normal-gap acidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap 140 - (16 + 94) = 30, a raised-gap acidosis. Change in gap 30 - 10 = 20; change in bicarbonate 25 - 16 = 9. The gap change is much larger, so a metabolic alkalosis coexists. The carbon dioxide of 30 is within the Winter range of 30 to 34. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 30, 50, 49"},{"q":"Laboratory results show pH 7.42 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 64 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 40 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). If carbon dioxide rises 0.75 mm Hg per 1 mEq/L rise in bicarbonate, which disorders are present?","choices":["Metabolic and respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis and respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis, compensated","Chronic respiratory acidosis, compensated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bicarbonate 40 predicts a carbon dioxide rise of about 0.75 x 15 to 16, to about 51 or 52 mm Hg. The measured 64 is higher than predicted and the pH is normal: a concurrent respiratory acidosis on top of the metabolic alkalosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 24, 23"},{"q":"How is the change in anion gap calculated?","choices":["Calculated anion gap minus 6","Calculated anion gap minus 10","Calculated anion gap minus 12","Calculated anion gap minus 25"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Change in anion gap = calculated anion gap minus a normal gap, with 10 the accepted value. It is then compared with the change in bicarbonate, measured from an accepted normal of 25; the two should be equal in a simple metabolic acidosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 27, 50, 7"},{"q":"What causes the raised carbon dioxide of respiratory acidosis?","choices":["Decreased alveolar ventilation","Increased carbon dioxide production","Increased alveolar ventilation","Net loss of bicarbonate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory acidosis is an increased carbon dioxide due to decreased alveolar ventilation, never increased carbon dioxide production. The body then tries to raise bicarbonate by cell buffering and increased acid secretion, over different time periods. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 18, 31, 41"},{"q":"How is the Kussmaul breathing of severe metabolic acidosis described?","choices":["Shallow, rapid, anxious breaths","Pauses with gasping breaths","Deep, regular, sighing breaths","Shallow, slow, irregular breaths"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms of metabolic acidosis are mainly those of the underlying disorder, with compensatory hyperventilation. In severe acidosis Kussmaul breathing can be seen: deep, regular, sighing breaths that lower carbon dioxide as respiratory compensation. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"In a metabolic alkalosis, which finding points to a coexisting respiratory alkalosis?","choices":["Carbon dioxide does not rise as predicted","Carbon dioxide rises above the prediction","Carbon dioxide rises as predicted","Gap change above bicarbonate change"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Compensation for a metabolic alkalosis raises the carbon dioxide. When it does not increase as predicted and the pH is higher than expected, a concurrent respiratory alkalosis is present, as with liver disease and diuretics. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 29, 24"},{"q":"A metabolic acidosis has a bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is the expected carbon dioxide range?","choices":["19 to 23 mm Hg","27 to 31 mm Hg","31 to 35 mm Hg","38 to 42 mm Hg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x bicarbonate + 8, plus or minus 2. Here 1.5 x 14 = 21, plus 8 = 29, so the expected range is 27 to 31 mm Hg. A measured value inside it means metabolic acidosis only. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A metabolic acidosis has sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 100 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is the change in anion gap?","choices":["11 mEq/L","14 mEq/L","16 mEq/L","20 mEq/L"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anion gap 140 - (14 + 100) = 26. Change in gap = calculated gap - 10 = 16. The change in bicarbonate is 25 - 14 = 11, so the gap change is the larger of the two; a gap change larger than the bicarbonate change points to a concurrent metabolic alkalosis. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 27, 7"},{"q":"Hypokalemia in metabolic alkalosis may produce which findings?","choices":["Compensatory hyperventilation","Hyperkalemic weakness","Weakness and hyporeflexia","Deep, sighing breaths"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms of metabolic alkalosis are mainly those of the underlying disorder, but the hypokalemia it causes may produce weakness and hyporeflexia (decreased reflexes). Tetany and neuromuscular irritability are rare. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 42, 52"},{"q":"Which is the major titratable acid in the urine?","choices":["Hydrogen phosphate","Ammonium","Urate","Carbonic acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Because the kidney cannot excrete free hydrogen, it combines hydrogen with urinary buffers, ammonium and the titratable acids. The major titratable acid is hydrogen phosphate, and the amount depends on daily phosphate intake. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 13, 20, 21"},{"q":"How does the body compensate for metabolic acidosis?","choices":["Potassium moving into the cells","Breathing more to lower carbon dioxide","Breathing less to raise carbon dioxide","The kidneys adjusting bicarbonate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metabolic acidosis is an excess of acid to buffer or a net loss of bicarbonate. Compensation is respiratory: respiration increases to decrease carbon dioxide, seen as compensatory hyperventilation and, when severe, Kussmaul breathing. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 5, 41, 42"}]};
